{"abstract":"This article studies the making of a small fragment of China's social credit system by focusing on the evolution of digital mediation in the market for domestic service. We study the ongoing development and reconfiguration of various forms of digital mediation in the domestic service labour market from early local digital blacklisting attempts to the making of a nationwide app linked to the social credit system. We find that digital mediation so far mainly contributes to controlling domestic workers through the indicators included in the app. These indicators to a large extent reinforce existing categorisations of domestic workers. While the early blacklists were primarily market-driven, thereby resembling the platform economy outside China, the later nationwide system is closely linked to the central state and strengthens central state control over the market for domestic services.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Jiang, Qiuyu","Zeuthen, Jesper Willaing","Bislev, Ane Katrine"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-687239"],"filters":{"added":"2023-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Digital mediation","Registration","Social credit system","migrant labour"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"finding good help reconfiguring digital mediation in china s domestic service labour market","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/18681026231167894","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-687239","identifier":[{"id":"85162961761","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-ef321095-a659-4e72-9890-1ae7497873de"],"keyword":["Domestic service labour market","digital mediation","migrant labour","registration","social credit system"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D25659A5E1E37EFE1E2EA165C353B9B1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/18681026231167894","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Politics and Society","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Politics and Society, MIX - Center for Displacement, Migration and Integration","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Politics and Society, REGIS - Regional and International Studies research group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Qiuyu","key":"jiangpau","last":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Qiuyu","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper Willaing","key":"zeuthenpau","last":"Zeuthen","locid":"85083652-d0e8-4727-b782-73f5646905ee","name":"Zeuthen, Jesper Willaing","orcid":"0000-0002-6780-7568","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Ane","key":"bislevpau","last":"Bislev","locid":"eb00c585-af62-42eb-8100-3dabff41f6ca","name":"Bislev, Ane Katrine","name_org":"Bislev, Ane","orcid":"0000-0003-0482-8887","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/18681026231167894","issn":["18681026","18684874"],"issn_org":["18681026"],"journal":"Journal of Current Chinese Affairs","publisher":"SAGE Publications","status":"e","title":"Journal of Current Chinese Affairs"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85162961761"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["E-Published (ahead of print)"]},"sid":["aau-ef321095-a659-4e72-9890-1ae7497873de"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Finding Good Help : Reconfiguring Digital Mediation in China's Domestic Service Labour Market","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-687239","year":"2023","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Based on 14 months of ethnographic research, this article examines how African Muslim migrants build and maintain faith-based business ethics and how they apply these ethical business norms to help navigate their transnational lives in Guangzhou, China. Most of the African Muslim migrants included in the study are small-scale business owners who engage in semi-formal economic activities in both local and home markets. They face racial, cultural, and legal challenges on a daily basis. Unable to access formal means of support due to their precarious economic and legal status, many African Muslim small-business owners rely on informal business ethics to ensure a safe trading environment and mitigate risk. Their business ethics, I argue, are rooted in what I term “religious common ground” – the moral and ethical values shared among migrants from different Muslim groups. This article also explores the enforcing mechanisms of African Muslim small-scale business owners’ business ethics, such as mosques and co-religion business networks. This article concludes that there is no universal, standard code of conduct among African Muslim business owners in Guangzhou. Individuals among different Muslim communities have diverse interpretations of business ethics and practice them differently based on their nationality, ethnicity, religious habits, and socio-cultural backgrounds. This article contributes to a small but important literature that addresses the central role that religion plays in Muslim migrants’ business practices in a non-Muslim society.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Jiang, Qiuyu"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-687542"],"filters":{"added":"2023-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Jiang, Qiuyu"],"keyword":["Islam"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"faith based business ethics among african muslim small scale business owners in guangzhou china","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.14426/ahmr.v9i2.1317","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-687542","identifier":[{"id":"85168430866","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-2bd12c9f-9b36-4357-8cd6-27d0af00a263"],"keyword":["African migrants","Africans in China","Islam","Muslim migrants","religious common ground"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-81F36B95EFA2D6B3D44836768A15D83A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.14426/ahmr.v9i2.1317","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Arts, School of Culture and Society, School of Culture and Society - China Studies, subject","Aarhus University, Arts, School of Culture and Society, School of Culture and Society - Department of Global Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Qiuyu","key":"jiangpau","last":"Jiang","locid":"01816dda-4fa3-4b5a-b715-63f9158f9d96","name":"Jiang, Qiuyu","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.14426/ahmr.v9i2.1317","issn":["24107972","24116955"],"issn_org":["24116955"],"issue":"2","journal":"African Human Mobility Review","pages":"60-79","publisher":"University of the Western Cape","status":"p","title":"African Human Mobility Review","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85168430866"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-2bd12c9f-9b36-4357-8cd6-27d0af00a263"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Faith-Based Business Ethics Among African Muslim Small-Scale Business Owners in Guangzhou, China","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-687542","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Environmental, social, and governance (ESG) performance evaluation criteria have become increasingly important for Fintech as tools to assess a company’s ability to generate environmental and societal value. On the one hand, Fintech is well-positioned to take advantage of ESG in their business models. The application of digital technology to financial services generates a significant amount of financial data that can be both shared with and analysed by governments and investors to inform the development of ESG guidelines. On the other hand, Fintech businesses are also under compliance pressure to integrate ESG in their own financial reporting and operations, as well as to adapt to new performance assessment criteria. ESG becomes even more important as customers and investors in Fintech businesses pay increasing attention to ESG performance, and talent and founders focus more and more on being sustainability-conscious and impact-driven. This report provides an overview of the current ESG reporting status of Fintech businesses in Denmark and the European Union (EU). We focus on startups as they tend to be disproportionately burdened by compliance and investment pressure due to their limited resources. Our goal is to take a first step towards developing a collective ESG reporting model for Fintech startups that enhances their competitive advantage despite a volatile economic and regulatory environment. This report presents the current state of ESG reporting in the Danish Fintech sector as informed by wider international discussions within the sector. In it, we map out the five forces that shape the Fintech ESG landscape: 1) policy and regulatory authorities; 2) capital providers; 3) clients and customers; 4) talent; and 5) knowledge communities. We focus on ESG reporting needs, practices, and challenges from different stakeholders’ perspectives. Building on these insights, we suggest a collective approach to ESG reporting in Fintech that is adaptive to the dynamic nature of developments in both ESG and Fintech. The findings and insights are based on a series of interviews with various practitioners in the Fintech ESG sector conducted from February to August 2022 alongside an analysis of relevant documents. The document analysis includes both existing ESG regulations and venture specific ESG frameworks.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Wang, Cancan","Jiang, Qiuyu","Dittrich, Yvonne","Østerlund, Carsten Svarrer"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-683626"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Syracuse University"],"keyword":["Digital financial services","ESG compliance","ESG performance evaluation","Fintech","Sustainability in startups"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"esg in fintech an overview","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101319385/TR-2023-209.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-683626","ids":["itu-fc28080e-72d7-47e7-9a4a-715527905639"],"keyword":["Digital financial services","ESG compliance","ESG performance evaluation","Fintech","Sustainability in startups"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-ED9F9872DF4700D4F26FBC44201C0CDF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101319385/TR-2023-209.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digitalization, Democracy, and Governance, REFLACT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"syracuse university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Syracuse University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Cancan","key":"wangpau","last":"Wang","locid":"605b59ab-91b4-4f8e-bb12-df68ce0491c5","name":"Wang, Cancan","orcid":"0000-0002-9065-6884","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Qiuyu","key":"jiangpau","last":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Qiuyu","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Yvonne","key":"dittrichpau","last":"Dittrich","locid":"f2136570-f73c-446f-9fb0-6d9e461e2124","name":"Dittrich, Yvonne","orcid":"0000-0003-4757-3394","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Carsten Svarrer","key":"østerlundpau","last":"Østerlund","name":"Østerlund, Carsten Svarrer","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"publisher":"IT University of Copenhagen","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-fc28080e-72d7-47e7-9a4a-715527905639"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"ESG in Fintech: An Overview","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-683626","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Environmental, social, and corporate governance (ESG) reporting has become an important instrument for the sustainable transition of the next generation of business-startup. Nonetheless, poor ESG data quality impedes effective reporting, especially in domains such as Fintech where top-down ESG metrics may overlook pertinent material issues. This action research study applies a design probe in the form of the notion of an ESG data commons to explore possible strategies to improve ESG data quality in Fintech startup. By reporting on the initial results of an ongoing study of a Danish Fintech startup cluster, we develop a practice-based approach that highlights the changing processes, teleoaffective structures, and sociomaterial dynamics of ESG data commons. We contribute to information systems (IS) research in two areas. First, we contribute to the call for a data-driven approach to ESG reporting. Second, the study extends the IS design literature by applying data commons as a design probe.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Wang, Cancan","Østerlund, Carsten","Jiang, Qiuyu","Dittrich, Yvonne"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382240"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Syracuse University"],"keyword":["Action research","Data Commons","ESG","Fintech","Practice theory","Startup"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"becoming sustainable together esg data commons for fintech startups","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90464219/Becoming_Sustainable_Together_Revision_Final.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382240","ids":["itu-41ae3a75-fc4e-4140-9d41-cabaa9c4b267"],"keyword":["Action Research","Data Commons","ESG","Fintech","Practice Theory","Startup"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-8D0706EBD5725B0F41CF95864461A5B8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90464219/Becoming_Sustainable_Together_Revision_Final.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Digitalization, Democracy, and Governance, REFLACT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"syracuse university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Syracuse University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Cancan","key":"wangpau","last":"Wang","locid":"605b59ab-91b4-4f8e-bb12-df68ce0491c5","name":"Wang, Cancan","orcid":"0000-0002-9065-6884","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Carsten","key":"østerlundpau","last":"Østerlund","name":"Østerlund, Carsten","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Qiuyu","key":"jiangpau","last":"Jiang","locid":"0284519c-3320-48a2-8b0d-b4ae9351a403","name":"Jiang, Qiuyu","orcid":"0000-0002-6888-897X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Yvonne","key":"dittrichpau","last":"Dittrich","locid":"f2136570-f73c-446f-9fb0-6d9e461e2124","name":"Dittrich, Yvonne","orcid":"0000-0003-4757-3394","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["00000033"],"journal":"Proceedings / International Conference on Information Systems","status":"p","title":"Proceedings / International Conference on Information Systems","title_org":"Proceedings / International Conference on Information Systems (ICIS)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-41ae3a75-fc4e-4140-9d41-cabaa9c4b267"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Becoming Sustainable Together : ESG Data Commons for Fintech Startups","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382240","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Pigs are valuable large animal models for biomedical and genetic research, but insights into the tissue- and cell-type-specific transcriptome and heterogeneity remain limited. By leveraging single-cell RNA sequencing, we generate a multiple-organ single-cell transcriptomic map containing over 200,000 pig cells from 20 tissues/organs. We comprehensively characterize the heterogeneity of cells in tissues and identify 234 cell clusters, representing 58 major cell types. In-depth integrative analysis of endothelial cells reveals a high degree of heterogeneity. We identify several functionally distinct endothelial cell phenotypes, including an endothelial to mesenchymal transition subtype in adipose tissues. Intercellular communication analysis predicts tissue- and cell type-specific crosstalk between endothelial cells and other cell types through the VEGF, PDGF, TGF-beta, and BMP pathways. Regulon analysis of single-cell transcriptome of microglia in pig and 12 other species further identifies MEF2C as an evolutionally conserved regulon in the microglia. Our work describes the landscape of single-cell transcriptomes within diverse pig organs and identifies the heterogeneity of endothelial cells and evolutionally conserved regulon in microglia.","author_count":45,"contributors":["Wang, Fei","Ding, Peiwen","Liang, Xue","Ding, Xiangning","Brandt, Camilla Blunk","Sjöstedt, Evelina","Zhu, Jiacheng","Bolund, Saga","Zhang, Lijing","de Rooij, Laura P M H","Luo, Lihua","Wei, Yanan","Zhao, Wandong","Lv, Zhiyuan","Haskó, János","Li, Runchu","Qin, Qiuyu","Jia, Yi","Wu, Wendi","Yuan, Yuting","Pu, Mingyi","Wang, Haoyu","Wu, Aiping","Xie, Lin","Liu, Ping","Chen, Fang","Herold, Jacqueline","Kalucka, Joanna","Karlsson, Max","Zhang, Xiuqing","Helmig, Rikke Bek","Fagerberg, Linn","Lindskog, Cecilia","Pontén, Fredrik","Uhlen, Mathias","Bolund, Lars","Jessen, Niels","Jiang, Hui","Xu, Xun","Yang, Huanming","Carmeliet, Peter","Mulder, Jan","Chen, Dongsheng","Lin, Lin","Luo, Yonglun"],"cuid":["S02-099.85-029953"],"filters":{"added":"2022-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Asia, East","Asia, Middle East","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Chen, Dongsheng","Lin, Lin","Luo, Yonglun"],"country":["Belgium","China","Italy","Sweden","United Arab Emirates"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","BGI","BGI-Shenzhen","Binzhou Medical College","Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences","Chinese Academy of Sciences","KTH Royal Institute of Technology","KU Leuven","Karolinska Institutet","Khalifa University of Science and Technology","Lars Bolund Institute of Regenerative Medicine","MGI Tech Co., Ltd. (Biz)","Peking Union Medical College","Polytechnic University of Milan","Qingdao University","Royal Institute of Technology","Suzhou Institute of Systems Medicine","Suzhou University","University of Chinese Academy of Sciences","Uppsala University","Vlaams Instituut voor Biotechnologie (VIB)"],"keyword":["Angiogenesis","Atlas","Contributes","Differentiation","Gene expression","Health","MODEL","PROVIDE","Resolution","TO MESENCHYMAL TRANSITION"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"endothelial cell heterogeneity and microglia regulons revealed by a pig cell landscape at single cell level","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-31388-z","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["We thank the FACS core team from the Department of Biomedicine at Aarhus University for the help of FACS experiments and data analysis. We thank Trine Skov Petersen and Xi Xiang for their technical assistance. This project was partially supported by the Sanming Project of Medicine in Shenzhen (SZSM201612074, to L.B. and Y.L), Guangdong Provincial Key Laboratory of Genome Read and Write (No. 2017B030301011 to X.Xu.), Guangdong Provincial Academician Workstation of BGI Synthetic Genomics (No. 2017B090904014 to X.Xu.), CAMS Innovation Fund for Medical Sciences (CIFMS) (22021-I2M-1-061 to D.C.), the DFF Sapere Aude Starting grant (8048-00072A to L.L.), and the Novo Nordisk Foundation (NNF21OC0071718 to L.L.). J.K. is supported by AIAS-CO-FUND II: GA: MSCA:754513, Lundbeckfonden: R307-2018-3667, Carlsberg Fonden: CF19-0687, Kræftens Bekæmpelse: R302-A17296, A.P. Møller Fonden: 20-L-0317, Riisfort Fonden and Steno Diabetes Center Aarhus (SDCA). P.C. is supported by Grants from Methusalem funding (Flemish government), the Fund for Scientific Research-Flanders (FWO-Vlaanderen), the European Research Council ERC Advanced Research Grant EU- ERC74307 and the NNF Laureate Research Grant from Novo Nordisk Foundation (Denmark). Y.L. is supported by the Independent Research Fund Denmark (9041-00317B), European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation program under grant agreement No 899417, and the Novo Nordisk Foundation (NNF21OC0068988; NNF21OC0071031). We thank Hårby Slagteren IvS for providing the study materials. We thank the China National GeneBank for the support of executing the project under the framework of Genome Read and Write. We thank Professor Fred Dubee for providing critical comments and language editing for the manuscript. We thank the FACS core team from the Department of Biomedicine at Aarhus University for the help of FACS experiments and data analysis. We thank Trine Skov Petersen and Xi Xiang for their technical assistance. This project was partially supported by the Sanming Project of Medicine in Shenzhen (SZSM201612074, to L.B. and Y.L), Guangdong Provincial Key Laboratory of Genome Read and Write (No. 2017B030301011 to X.Xu.), Guangdong Provincial Academician Workstation of BGI Synthetic Genomics (No. 2017B090904014 to X.Xu.), CAMS Innovation Fund for Medical Sciences (CIFMS) (22021-I2M-1-061 to D.C.), the DFF Sapere Aude Starting grant (8048-00072A to L.L.), and the Novo Nordisk Foundation (NNF21OC0071718 to L.L.). J.K. is supported by AIAS-CO-FUND II: GA: MSCA:754513, Lundbeckfonden: R307-2018-3667, Carlsberg Fonden: CF19-0687, Kræftens Bekæmpelse: R302-A17296, A.P. Møller Fonden: 20-L-0317, Riisfort Fonden and Steno Diabetes Center Aarhus (SDCA). P.C. is supported by Grants from Methusalem funding (Flemish government), the Fund for Scientific Research-Flanders (FWO-Vlaanderen), the European Research Council ERC Advanced Research Grant EU- ERC74307 and the NNF Laureate Research Grant from Novo Nordisk Foundation (Denmark). Y.L. is supported by the Independent Research Fund Denmark (9041-00317B), European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation program under grant agreement No 899417, and the Novo Nordisk Foundation (NNF21OC0068988; NNF21OC0071031). We thank Hårby Slagteren IvS for providing the study materials. We thank the China National GeneBank for the support of executing the project under the framework of Genome Read and Write. We thank Professor Fred Dubee for providing critical comments and language editing for the manuscript."],"id":"S02-099.85-029953","identifier":[{"id":"85132918827","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000815536300017","type":"wos"},{"id":"PMC9232580","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"35750885","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-ee72aaaa-0e92-471f-9d4a-fcb95edd3d03","ku-c016f80b-4f6b-4276-8a09-b8cbaa203af0"],"keyword":["ANGIOGENESIS","ATLAS","CONTRIBUTES","DIFFERENTIATION","GENE-EXPRESSION","HEALTH","MODEL","PROVIDE","RESOLUTION","TO-MESENCHYMAL TRANSITION"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-1BC49242B1255FB62CB029DC9E025358"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467